<!-- SOC-AI - rule 100760 (level 7). Domain: threat_hunting,linux,post_exploitation,
     Naming convention and load-order trap: see rules/README.md -->
<group name="threat_hunting,linux,post_exploitation,">

  <!-- T1014 / T1547.006 - kernel module load or unload, captured by the
       init_module/finit_module/delete_module syscall rules. Complements command
       observation (insmod/modprobe already are execve events): a rootkit calling
       finit_module() from its own binary runs no command at all.

       KNOWN NOISE: a reboot loads several dozen modules and will therefore
       produce a burst of alerts. That is accepted - a one-off, explainable burst
       is preferable to being blind to a malicious LKM being loaded, which is the
       hardest form of compromise to detect once installed.

       LEVEL 13 -> 7 (2026-08-01). Loading a module is a NORMAL side effect of
       ordinary administration: `nft` alone pulls in the whole nf_tables family,
       and each of those modprobe calls opened a CRITICAL incident - that is how
       100760 ended up in case 75, as a side effect of the 100645 regex bug.
       MEDIUM keeps the trace, searchable and correlatable as a weak signal,
       while staying below MIN_LEVEL (12): the soc-agent no longer opens an
       incident from it on its own, so it can no longer drive an autonomous
       remediation by itself.
       A rootkit loading an LKM rarely does only that: the strong signals around
       it stay at HIGH (100630/100631 fileless exec, 100748 ld.so.preload,
       100654 security service tampering) and pull this event into their incident
       through correlation. -->

  <rule id="100760" level="7">
    <if_group>audit</if_group>
    <field name="audit.key" type="pcre2">^audit-wazuh-module$</field>
    <description>Probable rootkit: kernel module loaded/unloaded by $(audit.exe)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1014</id>
      <id>T1547.006</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

</group>
